WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.161
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Thursday, November 5.

2
00:00:09.161 --> 00:00:20.277
Three things today: North Korea's fourth weapons test in a week, Seoul expanding its anti-piracy mission toward the Strait of Hormuz, and a record current account surplus.

3
00:00:20.277 --> 00:00:32.174
That fourth test in seven days, a new air-defense missile and a cruise missile airport-strike drill, comes the same day Kim Yo Jong is signaling the door to talks isn't entirely shut.

4
00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:40.040
The Bank of Korea Governor departs tomorrow for a Bank for International Settlements meeting running through November 11.

5
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.545
First, North Korea's weapons tests are piling up fast.

6
00:00:44.545 --> 00:00:57.396
On Wednesday, Kim Jong Un's Missile Administration tested a new surface-to-air missile system, confirming what Pyongyang described as its "intelligent multiple guided mode" and target-identification capability.

7
00:00:57.396 --> 00:01:07.432
Separately, North Korea staged a long-range strategic cruise missile strike drill simulating an attack on an enemy airport using cluster munitions on a runway. N.K.

8
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:20.345
News found that images published by North Korea's state news agency were heavily doctored. Those tests follow short-range ballistic missile launches from the Sondok area of South Hamgyong Province on November 4.

9
00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:32.523
That makes four weapons tests in a single week. The same day those missiles flew, Kim Yo Jong issued a statement. She is the Director of the General Affairs Department of the Workers' Party of Korea.

10
00:01:32.523 --> 00:01:40.907
She said, and this is a direct quote: "Dialogue is clearly one of the options, but it does not necessarily serve our national interests."

11
00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:56.818
She branded the FBI's attribution of a 6.3 billion dollar Binance hack to North Korea as an extension of Washington's unchanged hostile policy, and said there is no need to sit down with, in her words, "untrustworthy and hostile people" absent a policy change.

12
00:01:56.818 --> 00:02:00.490
Unification Minister Chung Dong-young read that differently.

13
00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:13.586
He said that looking at Kim Yo Jong's statements over the past month, more than ten appearances, she has used the word "dialogue" for the first time, and he read it as a signal that the door is not entirely closed.

14
00:02:13.586 --> 00:02:26.070
What to watch: whether Pyongyang's combination of tests and conditional talk signals a negotiating posture or simply escalation. Either way, four tests in seven days is the tempo you need to keep in mind.

15
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:39.907
Second, Seoul is pushing its naval reach toward the Strait of Hormuz. South Korea approved expanding its Cheonghae anti-piracy unit's operational zone from the Gulf of Aden to waters south of Oman.

16
00:02:39.907 --> 00:02:45.744
The expansion adds a P-8A maritime patrol aircraft and an explosive ordnance disposal team.

17
00:02:45.744 --> 00:03:02.871
The Defense Ministry says Somali piracy has surged more than three times over the previous year, with coalition forces concentrated in the Gulf and Red Sea following the U.S.-Iran war and Houthi clashes since early this year, leaving the Gulf of Aden under-protected.

18
00:03:02.871 --> 00:03:14.995
The motion passed a vice-ministerial meeting Wednesday. It still requires National Assembly consent under South Korean law before the deployment can proceed. The move follows months of U.S.

19
00:03:14.995 --> 00:03:24.681
requests for regional support that Seoul had effectively declined. The lawmakers expressed support for Korean soldiers injured in a DMZ landmine blast.

20
00:03:24.681 --> 00:03:36.164
Experts say the Democratic sweep of Congress complicates a separate but related ambition: South Korea's plans for a nuclear submarine, which would require U.S. transfer approvals.

21
00:03:36.164 --> 00:03:47.710
Democratic control, those experts warn, makes that harder to secure. The key number: the Cheonghae unit's operational radius will roughly double, to approximately 1,930 kilometers.

22
00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.737
Third, South Korea's trade account just broke a record.

23
00:03:52.737 --> 00:04:06.028
The Bank of Korea reported that South Korea's current account surplus reached 61.5 billion dollars in September 2026, breaking the previous monthly record of 49.7 billion dollars set in June.

24
00:04:06.028 --> 00:04:15.074
The year-to-date cumulative surplus through September reached 340.7 billion dollars, more than four times the same period in 2025.

25
00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:36.367
The Korea Herald argues that Democratic control of Congress is unlikely to unravel the U.S.-Korea strategic investment framework, because bipartisan support for shipbuilding, semiconductors, and power grid industries provides structural continuity regardless of which party controls the legislative agenda.

26
00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:44.786
KOTRA's post-midterm analysis reached the same conclusion. That said, South Korean defense stocks tell a different story.

27
00:04:44.786 --> 00:05:01.626
The combined market cap of Hanwha Aerospace, LIG D and A, Hyundai Rotem, and Korea Aerospace fell to roughly 62 trillion won from a 52-week peak of 170 trillion won by the end of October, as the drone era reshapes what buyers are spending on.

28
00:05:01.626 --> 00:05:07.610
The headline number to remember: 61.5 billion dollars in a single month, a new record.

29
00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:22.300
Now to Pyongyang. State media's main story today is a statement from Kim Yo Jong conditioning any U.S. talks on abandonment of hostile policy, framing the FBI's Binance accusation as proof Washington hasn't changed.

30
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:31.461
The brief's bottom line captures it: North Korea confirmed its fourth weapons test in a week, Kim Yo Jong publicly conditioned any talks on a U.S.

31
00:05:31.461 --> 00:05:46.833
policy reversal, and Workers' Party International Secretary Kim Song Nam led a party delegation to Beijing, where he met China's Wang Yi and separately received a delegation from the Party School of the Chinese Communist Party Central Committee.

32
00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:49.970
Kim Jong Un has not appeared publicly in six days.

33
00:05:51.170 --> 00:06:00.925
The rest of the news, quickly. The Seoul High Court has set January 26, 2027 as the sentencing date for former President Yoon Suk Yeol's insurrection appeal.

34
00:06:00.925 --> 00:06:11.054
Special counsel sought the death penalty; Yoon maintained in his final statement that his December 3, 2024 martial law declaration did not constitute insurrection.

35
00:06:11.054 --> 00:06:23.356
Separately, the Seoul High Court upheld Yoon's election law conviction, leaving an 18-month suspended sentence intact. The court found his false statements violated voters' right to informed choice.

36
00:06:23.356 --> 00:06:29.881
If the sentence is finalized, the People Power Party may owe 39.7 billion won in campaign reimbursements.

37
00:06:29.881 --> 00:06:42.805
In the National Assembly, the ruling Democratic Party brought what it calls a "fabricated indictment special prosecutor bill" to the floor. The People Power Party responded with a filibuster, blocking a vote.

38
00:06:42.805 --> 00:07:02.316
President Lee Jae-myung visited a public redevelopment project in Seongbuk District on November 4 and ordered faster housing supply, instructing officials to allow conversion of private redevelopment projects to public ones and to strengthen incentives including floor area ratio bonuses for early-stage districts.

39
00:07:02.316 --> 00:07:14.370
South Korea's consumer price inflation stayed above the Bank of Korea's target in October, the Wall Street Journal reported, keeping pressure on the central bank ahead of its next rate decision.

40
00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:24.871
The Bank of Korea's current rate is 3 percent, set in October. Seoul plans to introduce cornerstone investor lockup rules to stabilize share prices after stock listings.

41
00:07:24.871 --> 00:07:37.858
A Seoul court rejected Coupang's bid to block a 423 billion won data breach fine, upholding the largest fine ever imposed on a single company in South Korea, over a breach affecting more than 37 million users.

42
00:07:37.858 --> 00:07:47.675
Brazil's first ambassador to North Korea in a decade, Ricardo Primo Portugal, has taken up his post in Pyongyang, confirmed by a Chinese Embassy announcement.

43
00:07:47.675 --> 00:08:00.164
A UNESCO evaluation body has recommended inscribing taekwondo as intangible cultural heritage representing North Korea, ahead of a final committee decision next month, with no South Korean involvement.

44
00:08:00.164 --> 00:08:13.710
38 North analysis documents a first public showing of a new North Korean stealth strategic drone at an October 30 exhibition, following Kim Jong Un's call for absolute superiority in drone technology at the same event.

45
00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:33.021
ArmsControlWonk analysis of October 30 imagery finds Nampho Port is very likely expanding its primary container storage yard by at least 45 percent, which the analysis flags as atypical since North Korea does not generally transport goods domestically in standard intermodal containers.

46
00:08:33.021 --> 00:08:49.574
The Lowy Interpreter argues that Ukrainian warnings of massive North Korean troop deployments proved overstated, with Pyongyang extracting military experience, revenue, and technology access from the Russia partnership at a smaller scale than predicted.

47
00:08:49.574 --> 00:09:03.051
Gallup Korea's October 27 to 29 poll put President Lee Jae-myung's approval at 39 percent. Democratic Party support stood at 35 percent, the People Power Party at 30 percent, and independents at 28 percent.

48
00:09:03.051 --> 00:09:13.454
Coming up: the Unification Ministry holds its "Peace On: dam" inter-Korean cultural event at Cheomseongdae in Gyeongju on November 7, running 11 a.m. to 5 p.m.

49
00:09:13.454 --> 00:09:18.230
The APEC Leaders' Summit is scheduled for November 18 and 19 in Shenzhen.

50
00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:30.303
On Capitol Hill. Senator Jon Ossoff secured re-election in Georgia. The brief notes he has been a vocal advocate on Korean Peninsula policy and Korea-related trade issues.

51
00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:33.800
That re-election keeps a pro-Korea voice in the Senate.

52
00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:44.610
Meanwhile, experts cited by Yonhap warn that Democratic control of Congress complicates both the nuclear submarine transfer question and prospects for a Trump-Kim summit.

53
00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:56.852
Beyond the region. North Korea told the European Union to "mind its own business" after the EU passed a human rights resolution targeting Pyongyang. South Korea joined the U.N.

54
00:09:56.852 --> 00:10:01.432
General Assembly resolution as co-sponsor for the fifth consecutive year.

55
00:10:01.432 --> 00:10:18.560
South Korea's Foreign Ministry also rejected Moscow's claim that U.S.-South Korea military exercises rehearse nuclear preemptive strikes, calling the exercises defensive, annual, and aimed at maintaining combined readiness against North Korea's nuclear and missile threats.

56
00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:31.916
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 5,384, down about 5 percent on the day. The won traded at about 1,344 to the dollar.

57
00:10:31.916 --> 00:10:46.290
Brent crude was at about 96.50 dollars a barrel, up about 2 percent. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Thursday, November 5. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
